From 00f7cfda6623dba99e4e501c523cd0e2d06989a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: colum <git@volf.co>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:55:35 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] dsv41: TF_DS_COMPRESS_SIDE (off by default) runs the
 round's compressor chain on a stream of its own, joined before the first
 reader

Switch: TF_DS_COMPRESS_SIDE ("1" or "on"; anything else or unset: off). Read once per process in Round.init (where the round's other per-process switches live, not in forward) and stored on the round; set alike on both ranks, like every round switch (an uneven pair is still bit-identical here - every forked byte moves with its launch, only the order across streams changes - but set alike is the convention). With the switch off there is nothing: no stream, no events, no launch changes, no env cost in forward; the bytes are the base's, byte for byte. With it on, one stream and two events are created at Round.init (before any round runs and before any graph capture).

What forks (per compressed layer, behind a switch): right after `try rd.mark(e, .a_in);` in the layer's attention - after the grouped attn_in linears (wq_a, wkv, and for a compressed layer the compressor's comp_wkv/comp_wgate) have been launched, so the compressor's inputs (rd.ckv/rd.cgate) and the mix's output x are ready - the chain forks onto its own stream:
- ratio 1: the RMSNorm (tri_basic.rmsnorm, rd.ckv -> rd.lat);
- ratio 2: scatterRows (rd.ckv -> the layer's raw_kv slots, idx = rd.g(.rslot)), scatterRows (rd.cgate -> raw_score), cuMemsetD8Async(ch.invalid, 0, 4), gatherRows (raw_kv -> rd.kvg, idx = rd.g(.gi), sources cs.slots * raw_rows), gatherRows (raw_score -> rd.sg), e.exact.compress2 (rd.kvg/rd.sg -> rd.lat2), then the RMSNorm (rd.lat2 -> rd.lat).

The fork: cs_fork is recorded on the main stream, the side stream waits on cs_fork, the chain is launched on the side stream (the kit's calls through a sibling tri.Tri carrying that stream; ops/memset/compress2 through the stream handle directly), then cs_join is recorded on the side stream. The join: where the chain is launched today (the `if (lay.comp_wkv != null)` block inside `_kv_idx`), the main stream waits on cs_join and the ratio-branch launches are skipped. The chain exists in exactly one copy (`compressorChain` in round.zig, selected by the fork's cs_out flag), shared by the forked and the unforked path, so the two cannot drift; the unforked path runs the same launches in the same order on the main stream as before the patch.

What does NOT fork, and why:
- idx_wk's grouped linear (prompt.grouped with rd.lat): grouped() hardcodes e.s (rotMany/glinear/cuBLAS ride the caller's stream), and the staging it needs (ch.gxh, and the split-K scratch ch.gz) is shared with the main stream. Forked, it could race the main stream's own grouped calls; it stays on the main stream, after the join. Same for the latent RoPE/fp4Store block that follows it (ropeHeads(rd.lat), fp4Store into cs.comp_codes / cs.idx_codes): after the join, untouched.
- the latent rope/fp4Store block is the first reader of rd.lat in the same region; it is untouched and runs strictly after the join.
- the indexer's q-side (ropeHeads(iq), fp4QdP2, rowmmWts(rd.x)), the L2 prefetch, mark(.a_q), and the TF_DS_KV_SHARD q_sent RDMA block: all read only x / qa / iq / rd.x (never rd.lat, rd.kvg, rd.sg, rd.lat2, cs.comp_codes, cs.idx_codes), so they stay where they are; they are the overlap the fork buys.
- everything that DOES read the chain's outputs - idx_wk's grouped + rmsnorm + ropeHeads + fp4Store, the latent rope + fp4Store, the indexer select block (iotaVis / indexKeys / topkSelect / indexScore, reading cs.idx_codes via the indexer's IndexK and rd.lat via the idx_k path), the candidate-pool ops, comp = .fp4{cs.comp_codes} and the sparse attention after it - comes after the join exactly as before, unchanged.
- the chain for a layer with no compressor does not fork (has_comp false; no cs_out; the join site is untouched).

Placement: the fork is one layer's worth of independent launches; the join is enqueued at the same point the chain used to launch, before anything reads its output. The fork and the join are inside one stretch (attention() for one layer runs within one stretch), so CUDA-graph capture (TF_DS_GRAPHS=1) spans both, with the same event-record/wait mechanism the mixes' side stream (useSide/joinSide) and the prefetch (usePrefetch/joinPrefetch) already use under capture - no cupGraphAddEventNode fiddling. Graph/capture interplay is constructed from that precedent, not observed on the pair.

The Sinkhorn side stream is untouched. The compressor's stream is its own (cs_fork/cs_join, waiting only on cs_fork) and shares nothing with rd.side/rd.join; the Sinkhorn path is byte-identical with the switch on or off. If the Sinkhorn side stream is in use, the compressor's stream queues behind nothing but cs_fork.

PhaseTimer marks the main stream; the chain's GPU time lands on no phase (the forked launches are not between two main-stream marks) and `rd.launches` will not count them either. With the switch on, the layer's phase profile shows its sections minus the compressor's time. Documented here because it is a profile-reading difference, not a correctness one: no one should read a smaller a_q/a_idx window as "the compressor got faster".

Bits identical: the fork is a reorder of independent launches (the chain needs only what the grouped attn_in linears produced; nothing in the window between the fork and the join reads the chain's outputs; the join orders every later read after the chain). exact.py must stay IDENTICAL with the switch on; if it does not, the fork is wrong and this does not ship. Gates on the pair, not run on this host (host-only patch): exact.py IDENTICAL; then tf-dsv41-lanes --profile / the served process's TF_DS_ROUND_PROFILE /metrics line (0123) - the layer's sections should show the compressor's time gone from the main stream's a_q window (unchanged total acceptance); check /metrics for the profile's presence.

Tests (host): zig build test (this host's flaky big binaries reported one phantom "failed command" on the first run; that binary passes standalone - 408/408 - and every .zig-cache/o test binary passes standalone on re-run; second full run passed clean). Type-check/build: zig build install native -Dfatbins=/home/colum/.cache/tf-dummy-fatbins succeeds (binaries rebuilt). Not run here: any pair, any smoke, exact.py, any GPU. Unverified on the pair: the bytes claim (it rests on the launch-order reading above, not on a run), and the graphs-capture interplay (constructed from the useSide/usePrefetch precedent, not captured or replayed here).

Host judgement calls: ONE shared helper (compressorChain) instead of duplicated chain code - one copy, selected by a bool, so the forked and unforked paths cannot drift; eager stream/event creation at Round.init rather than a lazy first fork (device resources bought once, before any capture, and only when the switch is on; env read zero times per round); dropCompress() added (events then stream, mirroring dropPrefetch/dropShard) next to the dropSide/dropPrefetch calls in Model.close and at the three layer_main.zig driver epilogues; dropCompress takes no Engine (dropPrefetch/dropShard's shape). No .cu change; no other env parsing; the only native.zig change is the one purpose-comment line.

Patches: this is 0125 in the decode-step-structure series.

